  "response_text": "Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by enqueueing each thread after it yields or finishes its time slice. The scheduler dequeues the next thread to run, and when that thread yields, it gets placed at the end of the queue. This ensures a fair rotation of threads.\\nThe main benefit of using a queue over an array is that you avoid manual index manipulation (e.g., (currentThread + 1) % numThreadsRunning). A queue naturally supports enqueue/dequeue operations, making the scheduler logic simpler and more efficient for dynamically adding or removing threads.\\n",
